From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: expose the H.264 hardware rotator

The H.264 secondary output combines power-of-two scale-down with clockwise
rotation.  Disassembly and hardware tests establish the mode bits for
0/90/180/270 degrees.  H.264 control bit 9 enables rotation when both scale
fields are zero, and 90/270 swap the output raster and stride.

Expose that unit through V4L2_CID_ROTATE.  Treat rotation like scaling for
private full-size reconstruction frames, capture allocation, format selection
and COMPOSE negotiation, so reference pictures never point at transformed
output.  Scale and rotation may be requested together.

A generated four-quadrant H.264 frame verified the output order for all three
angles and for half-scale plus 90 degrees on H713 hardware.

The rotator lives in the H.264 engine, so the control is meaningless for the
other codecs.  Do NOT express that by failing: state-dependent rejection from
s_ctrl defeats the control framework's own range probing, which walks the
control against the CURRENT configuration.  The default output format is
MPEG-2, so refusing every non-zero value there made the advertised maximum
unreachable and v4l2-compliance reported

    fail: v4l2-test-controls.cpp(517): invalid maximum range check
    test VIDIOC_G/S_CTRL: FAIL

Store whatever the range allows and make the value INERT instead: everything
that acts on rotation reads cedrus_rotation(), which reports zero unless the
output format is H.264, so a stale request can never arm the H.264 transform
path under MPEG-2, HEVC or VP8.  Selecting another codec with a rotation set
is likewise no longer an error -- it simply stops applying, and comes back
if H.264 is selected again.

The reallocation a rotation change forces is advertised by
V4L2_CTRL_FLAG_MODIFY_LAYOUT, and cedrus_buf_prepare() rejects a now-undersized
buffer with -EINVAL, so nothing is corrupted if a client ignores it.  Do NOT
reach for v4l2_ctrl_grab() to police this: a grabbed control cannot be set into
a REQUEST either, which is legitimate and which v4l2-compliance exercises --
doing so moved the "test Requests" failure to VIDIOC_S_EXT_CTRLS instead of
fixing it.  recon_fmt derives from the CODED size, so rotation never resizes
the reconstruction buffers.

Measured on H713, v4l2-compliance -d /dev/video0:

    baseline (this series' cedrus patches reverted)   48/49
    with this patch as first written                  45/49
    with this patch as it now stands                  46/49

The four-quadrant rotation matrix still passes at 0/90/180/270 after the
rework.  Two failures remain, "test Requests" and "test blocking wait", and they are
NOT caused by this patch.  testRequests only runs if it can find an INTEGER or
BOOLEAN control to drive (v4l2-test-buffers.cpp:2360); every control cedrus had
before this patch is a compound, U32 or menu type, so the suite has always
returned OK after testing nothing.  V4L2_CID_ROTATE is the first INTEGER
control cedrus has ever exposed, so the suite now runs -- and finds that
cedrus_request_validate() never checks that the current codec's controls are
present, queueing a request compliance expects a stateless decoder to refuse
with ENOENT.  The blocking-wait failure follows from that one: fail_on_test_val
returns before testRequests can clean up, leaving buffers allocated.  Fixing it
belongs in cedrus_request_validate(), not here.  The remaining
VIDIOC_G/S/TRY_EXT_CTRLS failure is likewise pre-existing and present with the
whole series reverted.  See docs/reference/v4l2-compliance-2026-09-15.md.

Origin: https://github.com/cstenger/allwinner-h713-mainline/blob/43b2cdd4aa1b161a281db8b10f02ce70085e2d42/patches/kernel/0110-media-cedrus-expose-h264-hardware-rotation.patch (0110-media-cedrus-expose-h264-hardware-rotation.patch, commit fe65505)
---
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.c
@@ -82,13 +82,56 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static int cedrus_s_ctrl(struct v4l2_ctrl *ctrl)
+{
+	struct cedrus_ctx *ctx = container_of(ctrl->handler, struct cedrus_ctx, hdl);
+
+	if (ctrl->id != V4L2_CID_ROTATE)
+		return 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * Accept anything the control range allows and never fail here.
+	 * State-dependent rejection from s_ctrl defeats the control
+	 * framework's own range probing: it walks the control against the
+	 * CURRENT configuration, and the default output format is MPEG-2, so
+	 * refusing every non-zero value there made the advertised maximum
+	 * unreachable and v4l2-compliance reported "invalid maximum range
+	 * check". Rotation is instead made inert for non-H.264 by
+	 * cedrus_rotation(), and the reallocation this forces on userspace is
+	 * already advertised by V4L2_CTRL_FLAG_MODIFY_LAYOUT: a client that
+	 * changes rotation must reallocate, and cedrus_buf_prepare() rejects a
+	 * now-undersized buffer with -EINVAL. Do NOT reach for
+	 * v4l2_ctrl_grab() here -- a grabbed control cannot be set into a
+	 * REQUEST either, which is a legitimate thing for userspace to do and
+	 * which v4l2-compliance exercises.
+	 */
+	ctx->rotation = ctrl->val;
+	cedrus_reset_cap_format(ctx);
+	cedrus_update_recon_format(ctx);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static const struct v4l2_ctrl_ops cedrus_ctrl_ops = {
 	.try_ctrl = cedrus_try_ctrl,
+	.s_ctrl = cedrus_s_ctrl,
 };
 
 static const struct cedrus_control cedrus_controls[] = {
 	{
 		.cfg = {
+			.id	= V4L2_CID_ROTATE,
+			.min	= 0,
+			.max	= 270,
+			.step	= 90,
+			.def	= 0,
+			.flags	= V4L2_CTRL_FLAG_MODIFY_LAYOUT,
+			.ops	= &cedrus_ctrl_ops,
+		},
+		.capabilities	= CEDRUS_CAPABILITY_H264_DEC,
+	},
+	{
+		.cfg = {
 			.id	= V4L2_CID_STATELESS_MPEG2_SEQUENCE,
 		},
 		.capabilities	= CEDRUS_CAPABILITY_MPEG2_DEC,
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c
@@ -98,7 +98,7 @@
 	 * the smaller picture userspace sees and would size the primary's
 	 * stride and chroma offset wrongly.
 	 */
-	cedrus_dst_format_set(dev, cedrus_scaling(ctx) ? &ctx->recon_fmt
+	cedrus_dst_format_set(dev, cedrus_transforming(ctx) ? &ctx->recon_fmt
 						       : &ctx->dst_fmt);
 
 	error = ctx->current_codec->setup(ctx, &run);
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
@@ -136,6 +136,7 @@
 	struct v4l2_pix_format		recon_fmt;
 	u8				sd_h_shift;
 	u8				sd_v_shift;
+	u16				rotation;
 	struct cedrus_dec_ops		*current_codec;
 	unsigned int			bit_depth;
 
@@ -273,6 +274,35 @@
 }
 
 /*
+ * The rotator lives in the H.264 engine, so a rotation requested while another
+ * codec is selected is remembered but inert. Everything that ACTS on rotation
+ * reads it through here rather than touching ctx->rotation directly, so a
+ * stale request can never arm the H.264 transform path under MPEG-2, HEVC or
+ * VP8. Storing it unconditionally is deliberate: rejecting the value in
+ * s_ctrl instead broke the control framework's own range probing.
+ */
+static inline unsigned int cedrus_rotation(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx)
+{
+	if (ctx->src_fmt.pixelformat != V4L2_PIX_FMT_H264_SLICE)
+		return 0;
+
+	return ctx->rotation;
+}
+
+/* 90 and 270 degrees exchange the output raster's width and height. */
+static inline bool cedrus_rotation_swaps_axes(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx)
+{
+	unsigned int rotation = cedrus_rotation(ctx);
+
+	return rotation == 90 || rotation == 270;
+}
+
+static inline bool cedrus_transforming(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx)
+{
+	return cedrus_scaling(ctx) || cedrus_rotation(ctx);
+}
+
+/*
  * Where the decoder writes and predicts from.  EVERY frame address goes
  * through here -- the current picture and every DPB reference alike -- because
  * they must agree.  While the VE is scaling, the capture buffer holds the
@@ -289,7 +319,7 @@
 	if (!buf)
 		return 0;
 
-	if (cedrus_scaling(ctx)) {
+	if (cedrus_transforming(ctx)) {
 		cbuf = vb2_to_cedrus_buffer(buf);
 		if (WARN_ON_ONCE(!cbuf->recon))
 			return 0;
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
@@ -153,7 +153,7 @@
 	unsigned int stride = ctx->dst_fmt.bytesperline;
 	u32 reg;
 
-	if (!cedrus_scaling(ctx))
+	if (!cedrus_transforming(ctx))
 		return;
 
 	cedrus_write(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_LUMA_ADDR, (u32)luma);
@@ -173,7 +173,12 @@
 	reg |= VE_SECONDARY_OUT_FMT_EXT_NV12;
 	cedrus_write(dev, VE_PRIMARY_OUT_FMT, reg);
 
+	reg = cedrus_read(dev, VE_H264_CTRL);
+	reg |= VE_H264_CTRL_SDROT_ENABLE;
+	cedrus_write(dev, VE_H264_CTRL, reg);
+
 	cedrus_write(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL,
+		     VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL_ROTATE(cedrus_rotation(ctx)) |
 		     VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL_H_SHIFT(ctx->sd_h_shift) |
 		     VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL_V_SHIFT(ctx->sd_v_shift));
 
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_regs.h
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_regs.h
@@ -576,6 +576,7 @@
 
 #define VE_H264_CTRL			0x220
 #define VE_H264_CTRL_VP8			BIT(29)
+#define VE_H264_CTRL_SDROT_ENABLE		BIT(9)
 #define VE_H264_CTRL_VLD_DATA_REQ_INT		BIT(2)
 #define VE_H264_CTRL_DECODE_ERR_INT		BIT(1)
 #define VE_H264_CTRL_SLICE_DECODE_INT		BIT(0)
@@ -616,6 +617,7 @@
 #define VE_H264_VLD_LEN			0x238
 #define VE_H264_VLD_END			0x23c
 #define VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL		0x240
+#define VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL_ROTATE(r)		SHIFT_AND_MASK_BITS((r) / 90, 2, 0)
 /*
  * Two independent 2-bit fields, one per axis: 0 = 1:1, 1 = 1/2, 2 = 1/4.
  * A field of 3 is not a valid eighth -- it produces a partial write.  There
